WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.033 --> 00:00:00.767
Former President

00:00:00.767 --> 00:00:01.368
Trump is

00:00:01.368 --> 00:00:03.903
no stranger to making a lot of comments

00:00:03.903 --> 00:00:05.105
that make a lot of headlines.

00:00:05.105 --> 00:00:06.072
Caught on tape

00:00:06.072 --> 00:00:07.007
between the Access

00:00:07.007 --> 00:00:08.208
Hollywood tape that emerged

00:00:08.208 --> 00:00:10.310
just before the 2016 election

00:00:10.310 --> 00:00:12.078
the recording of him asking

00:00:12.078 --> 00:00:13.179
Georgia election officials

00:00:13.179 --> 00:00:13.613
to, quote,

00:00:13.613 --> 00:00:15.015
find votes

00:00:15.015 --> 00:00:15.548
to help him

00:00:15.548 --> 00:00:17.951
change the results of the 2020 election.

00:00:17.951 --> 00:00:20.487
Now his history of potentially damaging

00:00:20.487 --> 00:00:22.722
audio tapes adding a new chapter.

00:00:22.722 --> 00:00:23.957
As we've been reporting this morning,

00:00:23.957 --> 00:00:24.858
federal prosecutors

00:00:24.858 --> 00:00:25.892
have obtained a recording

00:00:25.892 --> 00:00:26.226
of former

00:00:26.226 --> 00:00:27.894
President Donald Trump acknowledging

00:00:27.894 --> 00:00:30.063
he held onto a classified document

00:00:30.063 --> 00:00:32.132
about a potential attack on Iran

00:00:32.132 --> 00:00:34.167
after he had left the White House

00:00:34.167 --> 00:00:35.602
and also suggested

00:00:35.602 --> 00:00:36.236
in that recording

00:00:36.236 --> 00:00:37.737
that he wants to share the information

00:00:37.737 --> 00:00:40.040
but was limited in his ability to do so

00:00:40.306 --> 00:00:41.941
by his post-presidency ability

00:00:41.941 --> 00:00:43.843
to declassify records.

00:00:43.843 --> 00:00:45.645
Joining us now, CNN political analyst

00:00:45.645 --> 00:00:46.980
and senior political correspondent

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:48.748
at The New York Times, Maggie Haberman,

00:00:48.748 --> 00:00:49.849
make a great to have you with us.

00:00:49.849 --> 00:00:51.751
So the way this has been detailed

00:00:51.751 --> 00:00:52.919
to our team of reporters,

00:00:52.919 --> 00:00:54.120
when they broke this

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:55.622
about the recording,

00:00:55.622 --> 00:00:56.790
there are sort of three parts

00:00:56.790 --> 00:00:57.390
to it, right?

00:00:57.390 --> 00:00:59.559
The fact that there was this document,

00:01:00.126 --> 00:01:00.660
reportedly,

00:01:00.660 --> 00:01:01.995
that there were conversations about it

00:01:01.995 --> 00:01:03.263
and that the former president

00:01:03.263 --> 00:01:04.431
acknowledged in those moments

00:01:04.431 --> 00:01:05.598
he wanted to share it.

00:01:05.598 --> 00:01:07.934
But there were those limitations

00:01:08.435 --> 00:01:11.638
really gets to a number of the quote

00:01:11.638 --> 00:01:12.572
unquote explanations

00:01:12.572 --> 00:01:13.073
that we've heard

00:01:13.073 --> 00:01:14.374
from the former president.

00:01:14.374 --> 00:01:15.775
And these don't square.

00:01:15.775 --> 00:01:16.176
Now, look,

00:01:16.176 --> 00:01:17.010
there's been a lot of news

00:01:17.010 --> 00:01:18.845
breaks in these documents investigation,

00:01:18.845 --> 00:01:19.779
various news outlets.

00:01:19.779 --> 00:01:21.081
We've had some you guys have had some.

00:01:21.081 --> 00:01:22.382
This one is very meaningful.

00:01:22.382 --> 00:01:23.450
And it's a big deal

00:01:23.450 --> 00:01:25.385
for the reason you just said there.

00:01:25.385 --> 00:01:26.820
It gets to number one documents

00:01:26.820 --> 00:01:27.687
that were in his possession

00:01:27.687 --> 00:01:28.755
documents that he had with him

00:01:28.755 --> 00:01:29.556
at Bedminster,

00:01:29.556 --> 00:01:30.857
which is a different location

00:01:30.857 --> 00:01:31.691
than Mar a Lago

00:01:31.691 --> 00:01:33.259
where the search had been focused.

00:01:33.259 --> 00:01:34.727
But more specifically, whatever

00:01:34.727 --> 00:01:36.029
that document actually was,

00:01:36.029 --> 00:01:36.696
because Trump is not

00:01:36.696 --> 00:01:37.931
the most reliable narrator

00:01:37.931 --> 00:01:39.199
about what that document was.

00:01:39.199 --> 00:01:39.666
And, you know,

00:01:39.666 --> 00:01:40.233
we know

00:01:40.233 --> 00:01:41.301
and you guys have reported,

00:01:41.301 --> 00:01:42.836
Mark Milley did not actually produce

00:01:42.836 --> 00:01:43.670
this document. Right.

00:01:43.670 --> 00:01:44.871
What matters is Trump

00:01:44.871 --> 00:01:47.507
most is Trump saying again,

00:01:47.507 --> 00:01:49.776
I haven't heard the tape. No one has yet.

00:01:49.776 --> 00:01:52.412
But our reporting is also that Trump says

00:01:52.412 --> 00:01:53.580
something about the limits

00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:56.182
of his classification abilities.

00:01:56.182 --> 00:01:57.484
Know, he expresses

00:01:57.484 --> 00:01:58.218
some regret

00:01:58.218 --> 00:01:59.786
about not having declassified

00:01:59.786 --> 00:02:01.488
this particular thing

00:02:01.488 --> 00:02:02.622
while he was president.

00:02:02.622 --> 00:02:04.224
That undermines the excuse

00:02:04.224 --> 00:02:05.959
that they have made over and over again

00:02:05.959 --> 00:02:08.328
that he had this ability to do that.

00:02:08.328 --> 00:02:10.263
He automatically declassified everything.

00:02:10.263 --> 00:02:11.297
I saw Jim Trusty

00:02:11.297 --> 00:02:11.898
on this network

00:02:11.898 --> 00:02:13.199
last night, Trump's lawyer

00:02:13.199 --> 00:02:14.334
saying exactly that,

00:02:14.334 --> 00:02:15.702
and then specifically not answering

00:02:15.702 --> 00:02:17.670
whether this document was declassified.

00:02:17.670 --> 00:02:17.871
And.

00:02:17.871 --> 00:02:19.005
Yeah, and so I just

00:02:19.005 --> 00:02:21.074
I don't see that question, by the way, of

00:02:21.107 --> 00:02:22.642
is it now with the National Archives?

00:02:22.642 --> 00:02:23.309
That's right.

00:02:23.309 --> 00:02:24.677
And there's

00:02:24.677 --> 00:02:25.678
I understand why he does

00:02:25.678 --> 00:02:26.546
want to answer those questions,

00:02:26.546 --> 00:02:27.914
but then don't go on TV to say,

00:02:27.914 --> 00:02:28.581
I'm not going to sit here

00:02:28.581 --> 00:02:30.517
and not answer these questions.

00:02:30.517 --> 00:02:30.750
You know,

00:02:30.750 --> 00:02:32.652
other than making the client happy,

00:02:32.652 --> 00:02:35.221
this this tape is

00:02:35.221 --> 00:02:36.856
multiple sources have described it

00:02:36.856 --> 00:02:38.925
as very problematic for Trump.

00:02:38.958 --> 00:02:39.759
Now, again, you know,

00:02:39.759 --> 00:02:41.361
this investigation is still ongoing.

00:02:41.361 --> 00:02:42.795
We don't know where it's going to end up.

00:02:42.795 --> 00:02:44.464
But of all of the evidence

00:02:44.464 --> 00:02:45.698
that we know of

00:02:45.698 --> 00:02:47.500
this is the most damning I've heard of.

00:02:47.500 --> 00:02:49.469
And it reminds us there's so much

00:02:49.469 --> 00:02:50.036
we don't know

00:02:50.036 --> 00:02:51.304
about what prosecutors have,

00:02:52.605 --> 00:02:53.373
so much we don't know and

00:02:53.373 --> 00:02:53.840
so much else

00:02:53.840 --> 00:02:54.807
that could be out there,

00:02:54.807 --> 00:02:56.242
especially when it comes to recordings,

00:02:56.242 --> 00:02:59.679
because recordings were made often

00:02:59.679 --> 00:03:00.580
with the former president.

00:03:00.580 --> 00:03:01.648
Well, so actually,

00:03:01.648 --> 00:03:02.048
that's,

00:03:02.048 --> 00:03:03.049
I think, another piece

00:03:03.049 --> 00:03:05.118
that's really striking to me about this

00:03:05.251 --> 00:03:05.752
Number one,

00:03:05.752 --> 00:03:06.886
Trump is very paranoid

00:03:06.886 --> 00:03:07.987
about people taking notes

00:03:07.987 --> 00:03:09.522
and he's very paranoid about tapes.

00:03:09.522 --> 00:03:10.423
He was notorious

00:03:10.423 --> 00:03:12.225
when he was a businessman for claiming

00:03:12.225 --> 00:03:13.459
he was taping other people.

00:03:13.459 --> 00:03:13.960
People at Mar

00:03:13.960 --> 00:03:14.460
a Lago

00:03:14.460 --> 00:03:15.328
always thought that

00:03:15.328 --> 00:03:16.963
conversations were recorded.

00:03:16.963 --> 00:03:17.764
This was

00:03:17.764 --> 00:03:19.465
he knew that he was being taped,

00:03:19.465 --> 00:03:21.267
that his own aides were taping

00:03:21.267 --> 00:03:23.069
because they thought it was

00:03:23.069 --> 00:03:24.938
it was for a Mark Meadows book

00:03:24.938 --> 00:03:26.005
that there is an irony

00:03:26.005 --> 00:03:27.106
to the fact that this relates

00:03:27.106 --> 00:03:28.174
to Mark Meadows, who

00:03:28.174 --> 00:03:32.612
whose sort of existence has been central

00:03:32.612 --> 00:03:33.479
to investigators

00:03:33.479 --> 00:03:34.314
in various parts

00:03:34.314 --> 00:03:35.582
of the January six investigation

00:03:35.582 --> 00:03:36.583
and so forth.

00:03:36.749 --> 00:03:37.717
And he had not been a key

00:03:37.717 --> 00:03:38.851
part of the documents investigation

00:03:38.851 --> 00:03:40.086
that we knew of before.

00:03:40.086 --> 00:03:42.255
This is this is significant.

00:03:42.255 --> 00:03:43.489
But Trump was aware

00:03:43.489 --> 00:03:45.325
that they taped routinely his aides

00:03:45.325 --> 00:03:46.159
because they were taping

00:03:46.159 --> 00:03:47.193
all of these book interviews.

00:03:47.193 --> 00:03:48.528
My book interviews with him

00:03:48.528 --> 00:03:50.563
that same year were all taped.

00:03:50.897 --> 00:03:51.798
He was aware of it.

00:03:51.798 --> 00:03:53.366
So it's not as if

00:03:53.366 --> 00:03:55.101
this was some secret recording,

00:03:55.101 --> 00:03:56.903
but he has this impulse,

00:03:56.903 --> 00:03:57.804
you know, in certain

00:03:57.804 --> 00:03:59.405
settings to sort of show off.

00:03:59.405 --> 00:04:01.541
And that's really what this seemed like.

00:04:01.975 --> 00:04:03.576
You have some really interesting

00:04:03.576 --> 00:04:04.677
new, new reporting,

00:04:04.677 --> 00:04:06.779
you and Jonathan Swan in The Times

00:04:06.779 --> 00:04:08.648
about the particular interest,

00:04:08.648 --> 00:04:09.449
the special counsel

00:04:09.449 --> 00:04:10.183
in the federal probe,

00:04:10.183 --> 00:04:11.017
Jack Smith,

00:04:11.017 --> 00:04:13.319
has taken around Trump's mindset.

00:04:13.319 --> 00:04:14.921
When he fired Chris Krebs,

00:04:14.921 --> 00:04:16.089
this was after Krebs

00:04:16.089 --> 00:04:16.656
said, look,

00:04:16.656 --> 00:04:18.224
the 20, 20 election was the most secure

00:04:18.224 --> 00:04:19.392
election we've had.

00:04:19.392 --> 00:04:20.793
He was the top cybersecurity

00:04:20.793 --> 00:04:21.961
official under Trump.

00:04:21.961 --> 00:04:22.762
Why does Jack

00:04:22.762 --> 00:04:23.696
Smith want to know

00:04:23.696 --> 00:04:24.564
what Trump was thinking

00:04:24.564 --> 00:04:25.832
when he fired Krebs?

00:04:25.832 --> 00:04:26.432
Well,

00:04:26.432 --> 00:04:28.234
what we believe he's trying to do,

00:04:28.234 --> 00:04:29.836
as is the case of the documents case

00:04:29.836 --> 00:04:30.236
as well,

00:04:30.236 --> 00:04:31.504
and part of why this tape is important.

00:04:31.504 --> 00:04:32.372
It goes to mindset.

00:04:32.372 --> 00:04:34.440
It goes to what exactly he was thinking

00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:36.309
when he made certain decisions.

00:04:36.309 --> 00:04:38.478
And so is the idea, you know,

00:04:38.478 --> 00:04:41.414
that these subpoenas relate to personnel,

00:04:41.414 --> 00:04:43.383
office officials in the White House.

00:04:43.383 --> 00:04:43.650
You know,

00:04:43.650 --> 00:04:44.550
who would compile this

00:04:44.550 --> 00:04:47.754
dossier about, you know, misdeeds

00:04:47.754 --> 00:04:48.187
the Krebs

00:04:48.187 --> 00:04:49.722
supposedly committed, which, you know,

00:04:49.722 --> 00:04:51.224
I think a lot of people looking at

00:04:51.224 --> 00:04:52.425
it would have a different view.

00:04:53.493 --> 00:04:55.094
But that's basically

00:04:55.094 --> 00:04:56.462
something that Smith is looking

00:04:56.462 --> 00:04:57.230
at, is trying to figure out

00:04:57.230 --> 00:04:57.897
how the White House

00:04:57.897 --> 00:05:01.901
interacted with Krebs, with the DOJ

00:05:01.901 --> 00:05:03.670
as various actions were being taken

00:05:03.670 --> 00:05:04.704
by Trump

00:05:04.704 --> 00:05:06.873
in relation to his efforts to claim this.

00:05:06.873 --> 00:05:09.242
The other probe, the Jackson part.

00:05:09.242 --> 00:05:11.511
Yes, this is correct.

00:05:11.511 --> 00:05:12.245
There is Jackson.

00:05:12.245 --> 00:05:14.147
This team is up to a lot.

00:05:14.147 --> 00:05:14.814
And so, you know,

00:05:14.814 --> 00:05:15.281
the documents

00:05:15.281 --> 00:05:16.816
investigation is the one that is sort of,

00:05:16.816 --> 00:05:18.318
I think, the most distilled

00:05:18.318 --> 00:05:19.319
and narrow fact

00:05:19.319 --> 00:05:20.286
that because it's clear

00:05:20.286 --> 00:05:21.387
we know what we're talking about,

00:05:21.387 --> 00:05:22.789
the January six investigation

00:05:22.789 --> 00:05:23.756
into your question about

00:05:23.756 --> 00:05:24.724
why does he care?

00:05:24.724 --> 00:05:26.225
There are so many different offshoots

00:05:26.225 --> 00:05:27.360
of that investigation.

00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:28.494
And this is one of them.

00:05:28.494 --> 00:05:29.562
And part of that, too,

00:05:29.562 --> 00:05:30.963
in your reporting in this piece

00:05:30.963 --> 00:05:32.298
you also mention this quote

00:05:32.298 --> 00:05:33.333
unquote, loyalty

00:05:33.333 --> 00:05:36.002
test around that same time.

00:05:36.336 --> 00:05:37.337
That's important, too,

00:05:37.337 --> 00:05:38.438
when it gets to mindset,

00:05:38.438 --> 00:05:39.305
when it gets to

00:05:39.305 --> 00:05:40.873
where everyone was exactly

00:05:40.873 --> 00:05:41.407
and they have been

00:05:41.407 --> 00:05:43.743
asking questions about this loyalty test,

00:05:43.743 --> 00:05:44.477
which my colleague

00:05:44.477 --> 00:05:45.545
Jonathan Swan

00:05:45.545 --> 00:05:46.379
broke the news of

00:05:46.379 --> 00:05:47.547
when he was at Axios

00:05:47.547 --> 00:05:48.448
that this thing existed.

00:05:48.448 --> 00:05:49.849
But they were basically trying to

00:05:49.849 --> 00:05:51.050
with political appointees

00:05:51.050 --> 00:05:52.618
and Krebs was a political appointee.

00:05:52.618 --> 00:05:53.886
Trump had appointed him.

00:05:53.886 --> 00:05:55.655
Trump had the right to fire him.

00:05:55.655 --> 00:05:57.690
But, you know, trying to figure out

00:05:57.690 --> 00:05:58.358
whether this person

00:05:58.358 --> 00:05:59.292
was personally loyal

00:05:59.292 --> 00:06:00.093
or any random person

00:06:00.093 --> 00:06:02.228
was personally loyal to Trump versus,

00:06:02.228 --> 00:06:02.528
you know,

00:06:02.528 --> 00:06:04.364
the government at large is part

00:06:04.364 --> 00:06:05.865
of what Jack Smith's team has

00:06:05.865 --> 00:06:07.033
been asking questions about.

00:06:08.101 --> 00:06:09.102
Pretty solid byline.

00:06:09.102 --> 00:06:11.270
Maggie Haberman and Jonathan Swan.

00:06:11.270 --> 00:06:11.871
I'll take it.

00:06:11.871 --> 00:06:12.972
I'm really happy, right?

00:06:12.972 --> 00:06:14.841
Yeah, that's right.

00:06:14.841 --> 00:06:15.208
Thank you.

00:06:15.208 --> 00:06:16.476
This is really interesting, Maggie.

00:06:16.476 --> 00:06:17.477
We appreciate it.

00:06:17.477 --> 00:06:18.578
So let's unpack this more.

00:06:18.578 --> 00:06:20.079
This developing story

00:06:20.079 --> 00:06:20.880
we want to bring in, CNN

00:06:20.880 --> 00:06:21.547
national security

00:06:21.547 --> 00:06:23.015
analyst and former deputy

00:06:23.015 --> 00:06:25.351
director of National Intelligence,

00:06:25.351 --> 00:06:26.185
Beth Sanner.

00:06:26.185 --> 00:06:27.987
So nice to see you this morning.

00:06:27.987 --> 00:06:28.221
You know,

00:06:28.221 --> 00:06:29.789
the classified documents

00:06:29.789 --> 00:06:30.590
here in question

00:06:30.590 --> 00:06:31.491
or document in question

00:06:31.491 --> 00:06:32.258
was something

00:06:32.258 --> 00:06:33.693
as sensitive as plans

00:06:33.693 --> 00:06:35.661
on a potential strike on Iran.

00:06:35.661 --> 00:06:36.763
Do we know anything else

00:06:36.763 --> 00:06:37.530
about the document

00:06:37.530 --> 00:06:38.865
and who the president

00:06:38.865 --> 00:06:41.100
wanted to show it to?

00:06:41.601 --> 00:06:43.002
I don't know anything about this

00:06:43.002 --> 00:06:44.303
particular document,

00:06:44.303 --> 00:06:45.738
but I do know that it is

00:06:45.738 --> 00:06:46.973
General Miller's job

00:06:46.973 --> 00:06:49.208
as chairman of the Joint Chiefs of Staff

00:06:49.442 --> 00:06:52.044
to respond to requests by the president

00:06:52.412 --> 00:06:53.613
to provide

00:06:53.613 --> 00:06:54.781
military options

00:06:54.781 --> 00:06:55.281
for something

00:06:55.281 --> 00:06:57.650
that the president would want to do.

00:06:57.650 --> 00:06:59.352
I don't see the Joint

00:06:59.352 --> 00:07:00.420
Chiefs of Staff

00:07:00.420 --> 00:07:02.522
role as freelancing

00:07:02.822 --> 00:07:04.791
in proposing war plans.

00:07:04.791 --> 00:07:07.860
War plans are proposed and provided

00:07:07.860 --> 00:07:10.663
in response to a question.

00:07:10.863 --> 00:07:11.898
And of course,

00:07:11.898 --> 00:07:14.033
you would want to have that plan

00:07:14.033 --> 00:07:15.535
be pretty robust.

00:07:15.535 --> 00:07:17.403
I mean, this is what the Pentagon does

00:07:17.403 --> 00:07:20.673
shock and awe for an attack to

00:07:20.706 --> 00:07:23.342
on on an adversary like Iran.

00:07:23.709 --> 00:07:25.011
So I think that's probably

00:07:25.011 --> 00:07:26.212
what was going on here,

00:07:26.212 --> 00:07:27.747
but I can't say in particular.

00:07:27.747 --> 00:07:29.348
So let's listen to a few clips of what

00:07:29.348 --> 00:07:30.183
the former president

00:07:30.183 --> 00:07:31.918
has said about his handling

00:07:31.918 --> 00:07:33.419
of classified documents. Listen

00:07:35.021 --> 00:07:35.354
to this.

00:07:35.354 --> 00:07:36.889
It doesn't have to be a process,

00:07:36.889 --> 00:07:38.224
as I understand it.

00:07:38.224 --> 00:07:39.025
You know, there's different

00:07:39.025 --> 00:07:40.026
people say different things.

00:07:40.026 --> 00:07:41.694
But as I said, that doesn't have to be

00:07:41.694 --> 00:07:41.928
if you're

00:07:41.928 --> 00:07:43.362
the president of the United States,

00:07:43.362 --> 00:07:45.398
you can declassify just by saying

00:07:45.398 --> 00:07:46.332
it's declassified,

00:07:46.332 --> 00:07:47.767
even by thinking about it.

00:07:47.767 --> 00:07:49.836
I have no classified document

00:07:49.836 --> 00:07:50.403
And by the way,

00:07:50.403 --> 00:07:53.005
they become automatically declassified

00:07:53.005 --> 00:07:54.006
when I took them.

00:07:54.006 --> 00:07:55.441
When it comes to your documents.

00:07:55.441 --> 00:07:56.342
Did you ever show those

00:07:56.342 --> 00:07:58.277
classified documents to anyone?

00:07:58.277 --> 00:07:58.978
Not really.

00:07:58.978 --> 00:08:00.213
I would have the right to.

00:08:00.213 --> 00:08:00.580
By the way,

00:08:00.580 --> 00:08:02.849
they would have survived after.

00:08:02.849 --> 00:08:05.184
Not that I can think of.

00:08:05.184 --> 00:08:06.552
So presidents have the power

00:08:06.552 --> 00:08:07.687
to declassify documents,

00:08:07.687 --> 00:08:10.089
but there is still a formal process,

00:08:10.089 --> 00:08:10.590
correct?

00:08:10.590 --> 00:08:12.725
Is he half right here?

00:08:12.792 --> 00:08:14.327
What's what's your reaction?

00:08:15.728 --> 00:08:17.797
President Trump is absolutely right in

00:08:17.797 --> 00:08:19.565
saying that any president

00:08:19.565 --> 00:08:22.435
has the right to declassify any material.

00:08:22.768 --> 00:08:24.670
They are literally the men.

00:08:24.670 --> 00:08:26.973
They do have that authority in law.

00:08:27.673 --> 00:08:28.140
But

00:08:29.175 --> 00:08:30.743
the details here do matter.

00:08:30.743 --> 00:08:32.478
There's no such thing

00:08:32.478 --> 00:08:34.847
as automatic declassification.

00:08:35.081 --> 00:08:38.050
That, to me, sounds absolutely absurd.

00:08:38.751 --> 00:08:39.418
There are two things

00:08:39.418 --> 00:08:40.186
I'd like to say here.

00:08:40.186 --> 00:08:43.055
First, there should be a process

00:08:43.055 --> 00:08:45.791
about how you go about this for a reason.

00:08:46.058 --> 00:08:47.760
But first, there should be a purpose

00:08:47.760 --> 00:08:49.529
and no one's really talking about that.

00:08:49.529 --> 00:08:51.731
Like the idea

00:08:51.731 --> 00:08:54.166
that you would declassify a war plan

00:08:54.166 --> 00:08:57.036
or any of the hundreds of other documents

00:08:57.036 --> 00:08:58.337
that were found.

00:08:58.337 --> 00:08:59.839
There should be a purpose.

00:08:59.839 --> 00:09:00.673
For example,

00:09:00.673 --> 00:09:01.874
when President Biden

00:09:01.874 --> 00:09:03.643
declassified information

00:09:03.643 --> 00:09:06.579
to warn China not to sell weapons

00:09:06.579 --> 00:09:08.848
to Russia, that's a purpose here.

00:09:08.848 --> 00:09:10.683
I can't discern a purpose.

00:09:10.683 --> 00:09:12.385
So it shouldn't be a whim.

00:09:12.385 --> 00:09:13.819
Just because you can doesn't

00:09:13.819 --> 00:09:14.820
mean you should.

00:09:14.820 --> 00:09:16.322
But the reason for the process

00:09:16.322 --> 00:09:19.258
is it gives the Pentagon in this case,

00:09:19.458 --> 00:09:21.027
or the intelligence community,

00:09:21.027 --> 00:09:22.295
a chance to say like,

00:09:22.295 --> 00:09:24.430
oh, if this information gets out,

00:09:24.630 --> 00:09:26.899
lives are at risk. U.S.

00:09:26.899 --> 00:09:28.734
national security is at risk.

00:09:28.734 --> 00:09:30.670
We should not do that.

00:09:30.670 --> 00:09:33.439
And usually a president would respond

00:09:33.439 --> 00:09:36.475
and say, okay, or can we redact again?

00:09:36.475 --> 00:09:38.611
Because there would be a purpose.

00:09:38.611 --> 00:09:39.512
The intelligence community

00:09:39.512 --> 00:09:40.446
would have to figure out

00:09:40.446 --> 00:09:42.281
how do you extract

00:09:42.281 --> 00:09:44.283
someone whose lives is at risk

00:09:44.283 --> 00:09:45.184
or how do you

00:09:45.184 --> 00:09:46.786
how do you protect this information?

00:09:47.787 --> 00:09:48.554
Fascinating.

00:09:48.554 --> 00:09:48.854
All right.

00:09:48.854 --> 00:09:49.789
Senior national security

00:09:49.789 --> 00:09:50.590
analyst Beth Santer,

00:09:50.590 --> 00:09:52.592
thanks so much for all that context.

